WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.720
How does a process change

2
00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:03.980
state? A process is a

3
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.560
program in execution, including its

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.640
current execution state and resources.

5
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:10.760
The same stored program can

6
00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.860
have several separate process instances,

7
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:14.460
so the file on disk

8
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.700
and a running instance are

9
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.440
different concepts.

10
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.980
The process image includes code,

11
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:20.860
data, a heap and a

12
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.260
stack. Its process control block

13
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.920
records information such as the

14
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.040
process identifier, state, program counter,

15
00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.140
registers and resource information. These

16
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.160
records allow the operating system

17
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.040
to manage and later resume

18
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:35.700
the process.

19
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.660
Use five conceptual states: new,

20
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.840
ready, running, waiting and terminated.

21
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.840
Ready means eligible for a

22
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.760
processor but not currently executing.

23
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.760
Waiting means the process needs

24
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.660
an event, such as input-output

25
00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.520
completion, before it can make

26
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.020
that kind of execution progress.

27
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.960
After admission, process A joins

28
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.120
the ready queue. Dispatch moves

29
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.700
A onto the processor and

30
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.020
changes ready to running. Process

31
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.680
B can remain ready behind

32
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.020
it without being blocked on

33
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:04.440
a device.

34
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.200
Suppose a timer causes A

35
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.400
to be preempted. A moves

36
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.740
from running back to ready,

37
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.540
and the scheduler can dispatch

38
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.340
B instead. This is different

39
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.480
from A requesting input-output: preemption

40
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.200
does not itself create an

41
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.640
input-output dependency.

42
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.680
Now suppose process A runs

43
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.280
again and requests a device

44
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.040
operation that has not finished.

45
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:29.720
It changes from running to

46
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.660
waiting, joining the relevant device

47
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.460
or event wait list. The

48
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.560
processor can execute another ready

49
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.640
process while process A is

50
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.000
blocked.

51
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.240
When the device completes, A

52
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.520
moves from waiting to ready.

53
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.620
It does not leap directly

54
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.520
onto the processor merely because

55
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.940
the event happened. Dispatch is

56
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.240
the separate step that turns

57
00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:50.200
ready into running.

58
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.480
On a process switch, the

59
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.420
required context of the outgoing

60
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.400
process is saved and the

61
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.760
incoming context restored. For an

62
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.200
authored example, preserve A at

63
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.220
program counter one hundred before

64
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.820
executing B, then restore that

65
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.160
saved point when A resumes.

66
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.180
The exact saved structures depend

67
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:10.840
on the system.

68
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.920
Long-term scheduling controls admission, short-term

69
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.360
scheduling selects ready execution, and

70
00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.480
medium-term scheduling can suspend and

71
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.480
later resume resident workload through

72
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.420
swapping. A process that finishes

73
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.200
changes to terminated in the

74
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.820
conceptual model. The xv6 lab

75
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.960
adds implementation states such as

76
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.140
zombie until reaping.

77
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:32.780
Things to take note of.

78
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.880
Distinguish ready from waiting, preemption

79
00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.920
from blocking, and event completion

80
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.280
from dispatch. If an xv6

81
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.200
listing says runnable, it means

82
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.580
eligible for selection, not already

83
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.940
executing; show the state-changing event

84
00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:48.120
before writing your answer.
